This is not a bug in Newtonsoft.Json. It's a bug in your code, that in its essence is unrelated to Newtonsoft.Json. In your situation, Newtonsoft.Json is just the trigger exposing the bug in your code, but there could be any other triggers in Unity or in your code that might expose the same bug if Newtonsoft.Json were not the first to stumble upon it. Therefore, i would like to ask you to close this issue.

The problem is the overridden `Equals(object)` implementation of your `SmallVector2` type. Look at the argument type, it's `object`. That means anything (and i mean **anything**) is allowed to be passed to to the `Equals(object)` method to be compared against the SmallVector2 instance.

However, you ignore this and blindly/optimistically assume that whatever can be passed to `Equals(object)` is also an SmallVector2 instance. Hence, to fix your problem with your implementation:
```C#
public override bool Equals(object obj)
{
    if (obj is SmallVector2)
    {
        SmallVector2 wpos = (SmallVector2) obj;
        return wpos.sx == sx && wpos.sz == sz;
    }
    return false;
}
```
However, i do not suggest this as the solution. Since `SmallVector2` is a struct (a value type), i am actually confused about why you felt the need to attempt overriding `Equals(object)`. The default implementation of `Equals(object)` for value types would just do the same (but correctly) as what you try to do with your overridden `Equals(object)` implementation. (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/fundamentals/runtime-libraries/system-object-equals) As it is now, it's redundant/pointless. Unless you intend to do some custom equality comparison logic that differs from comparing each and every field in the struct, just get rid of the `Equals` override...

(P.S.: I am not the author/maintainer of Newtonsoft.Json and not involved in its maintenance. I am a mostly former user of this library who for reasons science can't explain still likes to address bug reports in its issue tracker...)

