After thinking about this some more, I now understand why the default time format string uses `FFFFFFF` instead of `fffffff`: Many datetimes don't have fractional seconds, and appending `.0000000` to datetimes that only have whole seconds would be unexpected.

For example:
```c#
DateTime dateTimeWithFractionalSeconds = DateTime.Parse("2017-11-21 05:55:27").ToUniversalTime();

// Prints "2017-11-21T05:55:27Z"
Console.WriteLine(JsonConvert.SerializeObject(dateTimeWithFractionalSeconds));

// Prints "2017-11-21T05:55:27.0000000Z", which is unexpected.
Console.WriteLine(JsonConvert.SerializeObject(dateTimeWithFractionalSeconds, new JsonSerializerSettings{DateFormatString = "yyyy'-'MM'-'dd'T'HH':'mm':'ss.fffffffK"}));
```

The upshot being that if you need your JSON datetimes to be sortable or comparable (e.g. they're being put into a JSON-backed DB, such as Azure Cosmos), you need to manually specify `fffffff` in your JSON settings:

```c#
var jsonSerializerSettings = new JsonSerializerSettings
{
    // The default JSON serializer uses this DateTime format string: yyyy'-'MM'-'dd'T'HH':'mm':'ss.FFFFFFFK
    // Using FFFFFFF means that trailing zeros in fractional seconds are truncated, which can lead to JSON
    // with dates of different lengths being emitted.
    // e.g.
    // 2017-11-21T05:55:27.064Z
    // 2022-03-18T09:32:91.1028371Z
    // 
    // Having dates strings of different lengths can cause the dates to be sorted incorrectly,
    // so to get JSON dates with consistent lengths, we override the date format string and use fffffff instead of FFFFFFF.
    // 
    // See https://github.com/JamesNK/Newtonsoft.Json/issues/2780
    DateFormatString = "yyyy'-'MM'-'dd'T'HH':'mm':'ss.fffffffK"
};
```

