If i understand you correctly, you want to exclude/ignore the _ProductType.Products_ property from/during serialization. ReferenceLoopHandling.Ignore is working just fine, it's simply not its purpose to do what you would like it to do.[^1] 

There are a number of different ways to exclude type members such as _ProductType.Products_ from serialization.

The first and simplest way is attribute-based. Newtonsoft.Json offers a number of attributes to either either explicitly exclude/ignore certain properties from being serialized (opt-out), or explictly define which properties should partake in serialization (opt-in). 

If you don't like to make your data types/classes dependent on Newtonsoft.Json attributes, Newtonsoft.Json also supports certain attributes from the .NET base class libary (like DataContract/DataMember), for example.

For an overview of what the supported attributes are, see the documentation: https://www.newtonsoft.com/json/help/html/SerializationAttributes.htm

In the simplest case, your _ProductType_ class could be like this:
```#C
public class ProductType
{
    [Key]
    public int Id { get; set; }

    [JsonIgnore]        <=============== note this
    public virtual ICollection<Product> Products{ get; set; }
}
```

There are other ways to exclude the _ProductType.Products_, like defining a [_ShouldSerialize*_ method](https://www.newtonsoft.com/json/help/html/ConditionalProperties.htm), although the attribute-based approach would be simpler for a non-conditional exclusion of _ProductType.Products_.

If you can't or don't want to modify your _ProductType_ class, you could also write a custom ContractResolver that excludes the  _ProductType.Products_ property from serialization. An example of how to do that can be found in the documentation: https://www.newtonsoft.com/json/help/html/ConditionalProperties.htm#ID0EAAABAAA_tab1 (it's the example titled "_Conditional properties with IContractResolver_"). Basically, for your situation, such a contract resolver could perhaps boil down to:
```C#
public class NoProductsInProductTypeContractResolver : DefaultContractResolver
{
    protected override JsonProperty CreateProperty(MemberInfo member, MemberSerialization memberSerialization)
    {
        JsonProperty property = base.CreateProperty(member, memberSerialization);

        if (property.DeclaringType == typeof(ProductType) && property.PropertyName == nameof(ProductType.Products))
        {
            property.Ignored = true;
        }

        return property;
    }
}
```

[^1]: If my understanding of your situation is correct, no wonder ReferenceLoopHandling won't help you here. The setting is not about whether an object is referenced by more than one other object or about some other arbitrary relationships between objects, but specifically about reference loops (in other words circular object graphs). If you look at the serialized result you got, you'll notice that the serializer ignores (hence the name _ReferenceLoopHandling.Ignore_) following _only_ those object references that would complete/close/create a circular loop that would otherwise lead to "infinite" recursion (which would in actuality not be infinite but end with either a stack overflow or an out of memory error during serialization; the former being a death sentence for the affected thread or program it cannot recover from and the latter can also be hard to recover from).

