That's because the chosen property name "_$id_" clashes with the metadata keyword `$id`. Newtonsoft.Json recognizes this (and other) metadata keywords by default when deserializing json (and is unfortunately very poorly documented by Newtonsoft.Json's documentation).

As you noticed, this can be a problem when you construct Json objects which have property names that match those metadata keywords but should be rather treated as ordinary property names.

In cases such as this where the property names in constructed Json objects are or cannot be explicitly controlled and defined or clashes with metadata keywords cannot be avoided, recognition of metadata keywords such as `$id` can be disabled, effectively turning them into ordinary property names as far as Newtonsoft.Json is concerned.

To disable the metadata keyword recognition, set the `JsonSerializerSettings.MetadataPropertyHandling` property to [`MetadataPropertyHandling.Ignore`](https://www.newtonsoft.com/json/help/html/T_Newtonsoft_Json_MetadataPropertyHandling.htm), for example like this:
```C#
var serializationSettings = new JsonSerializerSettings
{
    MetadataPropertyHandling = MetadataPropertyHandling.Ignore
};
		
JsonConvert.DefaultSettings = () => serializationSettings;
```

