I would suggest the following approach:

1. Convert the object graph you want to serialize into a Newtonsoft.Json.Linq.JObject representation using the [JObject.FromObject]() method. This method also accepts a _JsonSerializer_, this way you can maintain the JsonSerializationSettings of your choosing.
2. A _JObject_ is an enumerable of JProperty instances, which contain the name and the value of each property in the JObject. (A JObject is also an enumerable of KeyValuePairs carrying the same information, btw.)
3. Iterate over all those JProperty instances whose value are JObject instances.
4. Recursively apply the described approach to the JObject instances of each property.
5. After the described approach has been applied to the JObject instance of a property, check whether this JObject still has any children (in other words, still possesses any properties).
6. If the property's JObject value instance has no children (i.e., has no properties), remove the property.
7. Continue with the next property.

So, basically this should boil down to a method similar to:
```C#
static void RemoveEmptyObjectProperties(JObject jobj)
{
	var propertiesWithObjectValues =
		jobj
		.OfType<JProperty>()
		.Where(p => p.Value is JObject);

	//    
	// Since we are going to manipulate the JObject while we are iterating the properties of said JObject,
 	// it is probably safer to iterate over a copy of the property list instead of directly iterating over
        //  the JObject while it is being manipulated.
	//    
	propertiesWithObjectValues = propertiesWithObjectValues.ToList();

	foreach (var jprop in propertiesWithObjectValues)
	{
		var propValue = (JObject) jprop.Value;
		RemoveEmptyObjectProperties(propValue);
		if (propValue.Count == 0)
			jobj.Remove(jprop.Name);
	}
}
```
