@elgonzo good thing I have e-mail notifications turned on. For the benefit of everyone who has the same problem:

> The problem is due to your contract resolver relying on the DefaultContractResolver collecting the instance properties and calling the CreateProperty override in your DatabaseEntryResolver. Since B.Id hides A.Id (in case you are not aware, note that member hiding is an entirely different concept from member overriding, and B.Id does not override A.Id), the A.Id is not part of the properties collected by the logic as implemented by DefaultContractResolver when serializing an object of type B (note that your example code given in the StackOverflow question mistakenly uses an instance of A instead of B)
> When serializing an object of type B, the only properties collected are B.Id, B.Name, and A. CompKey. Note that A.Id is not being collected because it is hidden by B.Id.

> What you need to do (conceptually) in your contract resolver is to additionally override GetSerializableMembers, with the following logic (pseudo-code):

```csharp
protected override List<MemberInfo> GetSerializableMembers(Type objectType)
{
	var members = base.GetSerializableMembers(objectType);
	var baseType = objectType.BaseType;
	while (baseType != null)
	{
		var baseMembers = base.GetSerializableMembers(baseType);

		baseMembers = filter baseMembers based on the member being a property or field and such

		members = Union of members and baseMembers -- make sure the same member does not appear multiple times in the members collection

		baseType = baseType.BaseType;
	}

	return members;
}
```

Yeah, it's unfortunately because Json.NET (incorrectly, in my opinion) uses the roundtrip format specifier "R" for `double` conversions to strings in the respective JsonConvert methods, like: https://github.com/JamesNK/Newtonsoft.Json/blob/e44ab3348fabcbe9ed6b5053b706a9e10b945ba1/Src/Newtonsoft.Json/JsonConvert.cs#L293-L301
As per documentation at https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/base-types/standard-numeric-format-strings#round-trip-format-specifier-r, "R" can fail to roundtrip certain `double` values. As per the same documentation, it would be better to use the "G17" for `double` and "G9" for `float` instead of "R".

It should be a rather straightforward and quick fix to change the respective float/double-to-string conversion in JsonConvert and elsewhere in the library. However, since this would alter the behavior of the library in some way, i leave it to the author of Json.NET to decide whether and how to address this issue.

As of now, the probably easiest workaround for serializing `double` with full precision is to use a custom JsonConverter that converts `double` values to a string with `.ToString("G17")` and write the converted string value raw to the JsonWriter provided to the converter, kinda like:
```c#
public class FullPrecisionDoubleConverter : JsonConverter<double>
{
    public override bool CanRead => false;

    public override void WriteJson(JsonWriter writer, double value, JsonSerializer serializer)
    {
        var s = value.ToString("G17", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
        writer.WriteRawValue(s);
    }

    public override double ReadJson(JsonReader reader, Type objectType, double existingValue, bool hasExistingValue, JsonSerializer serializer)
    {
        throw new NotSupportedException();
    }
}
```
