Yeah, from the look of it, it seems to be a bug / design mistake in `DefaultContractResolver.GetImmutableConstructor`:

https://github.com/JamesNK/Newtonsoft.Json/blob/e44ab3348fabcbe9ed6b5053b706a9e10b945ba1/Src/Newtonsoft.Json/Serialization/DefaultContractResolver.cs#L629-L656

Instead of iterating over all available parameterized constructors of immutable types (such as your structs) to find one whose arguments match the properties, it just picks the first constructor as provided through reflection (and there is no guarantee in which order the constructors are being returned by the Type.GetConstructors method). And if the arguments of that "first" constructor don't match the properties it's game over, despite there possibly being other public constructors whose arguments would match the properties. Well, and if that is not enough, even if that "first" constructor would match, the method here would still return `null` if there are more than a single public constructor declared in the type (don't ask me why). Bumma!
<br>

There are basically two ways out of this hole: Either annotate the appropiate constructors in your immutable structs with `[JsonConstructor]`  attributes, kind like:

```c#
public struct Rec2i
{
    public Vec2i Pos  { get;}
    public Vec2i Size { get;}

    public Rec2i(int x, int y, int w, int h)
    {
        Pos  = new Vec2i(x, y);
        Size = new Vec2i(w, h);
    }

    [JsonConstructor]
    public Rec2i(Vec2i pos, Vec2i size)
    {
        Pos  = pos;
        Size = size;
    }

       ...
}
```

Or, if annotating your data models with Newtonsoft.Json-specific attributes is not desirable, derive your own ContractResolver from DefaultContractResolver that selects the correct constructor for your immutable structs. For getting a basic idea of how to do this, look at some answers of this Stackoverflow question: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23017716/json-net-how-to-deserialize-without-using-the-default-constructor But note that the contract resolvers given in those answers are not necessarily ready-to-use -- you probably need to adapt them so that the constructors that are correct for your use case are selected.
<BR>

P.S.: When you choose to implement a custom constract resolver, I do NOT suggest to rely on the order of constructors in your source code and in what order the constructors are returned by the Type.GetConstructors() method. There is no guarantee whatsoever in which order of the constructors are provided by this method...
<br>

