What is the business value to this? `Class` (and `Record`) definitions already define the _default_ state of the object that is instantiated before the properties are set during deserialization. 
Serialization and Deserialization should be repeatable, the process should not inject information that was not already there.

Consider the following class:

    public class Student
    {
        public string Name { get;set; }
        public Student OlderSibling { get;set; }
    } 

The serialized state for a single record might look like this

    { "Name": "Test Kid" }

With your `MissingMemberHandling.Default` the deserialized object, if serialized might now look like this:

    { 
        "Name": "Test Kid",
        "OlderSibling": { } 
    }

But now if we deserialize this, we start to get more interesting results, in this case the `Name` field will be initialized to an empty string, because it was missing, and it will have another older sibling:

    { 
        "Name": "Test Kid",
        "OlderSibling": {
            "Name": "",
            "OlderSibling": { }
         } 
    }

Where might this issue present itself? If your application allowed a `Student` to be loaded from a serialized source, then after making changes to _other fields_ they then save this record to a serialized target. Now imagine the user loads and saves the same record many times, suddenly "Test Kid", who is actually an only child in real life, now has a lot of siblings in the application.

Is this a good design to encapsulate a _Student_, not by far... But only the _Author_ of the `Student` class and what ever parent structure this record is referenced from knows what the intent of this design was, the definition of the class itself is an expression of that intent, so it is within the definition of the class that the instructions on how to manage the serialization and deserialization should be defined, or in the business logic that manages the serialization process.

Right now, if you actually _wanted_ this behaviour, you would define your class with an initializer for the property that you want to be pre-initialized, noting that this only works if `MissingMemberHandling.Ignore` is set:

    public class Student
    {
        public string Name { get;set; }
        public Student OlderSibling { get;set; } = new Student();
    } 

> you could also define this in the default constructor for the class, IMO that is a habit that leads to maintenance issues down the track and is harder to infer from the model during design time when you are using this class.

In this fictitious class, it might be acceptable for `Name`  to be pre-init, but not `OlderSibling`, but a `MissingMemberHandling.Default` would not be able to differentiate between different members, it is all or none.

A more acceptable `Student` class might be this:

    public class Student
    {
        public string Name { get;set; } = String.Empty;
        public Student OlderSibling { get;set; }
    } 

Now the deserializing an empty JSON object, and then serializing would look like this:

    { "Name": "" }

If you wanted specifically to manage deserialization for specific properties or types, you could always use one of the deserialization call backs:

    public class Student
    {
        public string Name { get;set; }
        public Student OlderSibling { get;set; }
        
        [OnDeserializing]
        internal void OnDeserializingMethod(StreamingContext context)
        {
            Name = "Default Student";
        }
    } 

