The answer given is technically not correct. Yes, with regard to the example code given, making the Movie class and the property setters public is a possible approach. But it doesn't mean Json.NET requires you to do so.

If you prefer to keep the type and the property setter internal, then it is still possible to (de)serialize. In this case, however, the type and the members participating in (de)serialization need to be decorated by `[JsonProperty]` or the `[DataContract]`/`[DataMember]` attributes to guide Json.NET, for example:

```
using Newtonsoft.Json;

internal class Movie
{
    [JsonProperty]
    public string Name { get; internal set; }

    [JsonProperty]
    public string ReleaseDate { get; internal set; }
}
```
or
```
using System.Runtime.Serialization;

[DataContract]
internal class Movie
{
    [DataMember]
    public string Name { get; internal set; }

    [DataMember]
    public string ReleaseDate { get; internal set; }
}
```
**Important:** To be able to use the `[JsonProperty]`, you will have to import the namespace `Newtonsoft.Json` (if not done so already). The `[DataContract]` and `[DataMember]` attributes will require you to import the `System.Runtime.Serialization` namespace.

-----
Side note: If you foresee the possibility, however small, that you will switch to another (de)serialization library in the future, i suggest to make/keep the types and members participating in (de)serialization public. Unlike Json.NET, not every other serialization library can handle internal/private types and property getters/setters as easily as Json.NET, if at all.

