Work-around: `JToken` has another overload of `ToString`:

    public string ToString(
    	Formatting formatting,
    	params JsonConverter[] converters
    )

This overload will always return JSON.

My interpretation of this is that it is no longer important whether a string `JValue` was originally intended to return an encoded or unencoded string from `.ToString()`. The problem is that the library has been around for many years and I'm certain _many_ people have written code that depends on `.ToString()`ing a string `JValue`. If indeed it is a bug that `.ToString()` didn't return an encoded JSON string, well, it can't be fixed now without breaking tons of users' code. So, instead, the "fix" to bring the code and the contract into alignment must be to alter the contract, as unsatisfying as it is. The presence of a clear work-around means that you _can_ write code that depends on the behaviour the contract used to guarantee -- you just have to call a slightly different overload.

The work-around I suggested and the work-around added to the documentation both take a `converters` parameter, but it is a `params` parameter; it is valid syntax to supply no arguments for it. To get the alternate `JToken.ToString()` overload, thus, all you need to supply is one of the `Formatting` enum values.

The documentation suggests using `WriteTo()` to get the JSON. The `JToken.ToString()` overload that I suggested as a work-around just creates an in-memory buffer and calls `WriteTo`, so it amounts to the same thing.

```
  var x = new JValue("string value");

  var a = x.ToString(); // "string value"
  var b = x.ToString(Formatting.Indented); // "\"string value\""

  // And this is effectively what's going on behind the scenes of the calculation of 'b':
  var buffer = new StringWriter();
  var writer = new JsonTextWriter(buffer);

  writer.Formatting = ...; // (same value as passed into .ToString())

  x.WriteTo(new JsonTextWriter(buffer));

  var c = buffer.ToString(); // "\"string value\""
```

Using this `JToken.ToString` overload means that _any_ `JToken` value will be converted to a JSON representation, providing a generalized "Get me the JSON string" method.

Note that if you're just going to take that string and write it to a stream, you _should_ use `WriteTo` instead and avoid the intermediate string representation.

