I also had to set `TypeNameAssemblyFormatHandling` to `TypeNameAssemblyFormatHandling.Full` because https://dotnetfiddle.net/ executes user code in partial trust which restricts loading of types by name to fully qualified assembly names.

However, neither of these changes should affect your issue.

Incidentally, you have a security flaw in `Deserialize()`: you should deserialize to the base type `LogEntry` rather than to something untyped.  Your current code allows an attacker to trick your code into instantiating an *attack gadget*.  For why, see 

 - [TypeNameHandling caution in Newtonsoft Json](https://stackoverflow.com/q/39565954/3744182).
 -  https://www.blackhat.com/docs/us-17/thursday/us-17-Munoz-Friday-The-13th-JSON-Attacks-wp.pdf
 - [External json vulnerable because of Json.Net TypeNameHandling auto?](https://stackoverflow.com/a/49040862/3744182).

The security risk can be ameliorated by using typed deserialization:

		public static LogEntry Deserialize(string json)
		{
			return JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<LogEntry>(
				json, new JsonSerializerSettings()
				{
					TypeNameHandling = TypeNameHandling.All
				});
		}

I was able to reproduce your problem in a fiddle [here](https://dotnetfiddle.net/hnZCsU).

Your problem is that your derived class `LE2` does not contain a parameterized constructor that allows `TimeStamp` to be set, e.g. as shown below:

    public class LE2 : LogEntry2
    {
        public LE2(DateTime? timeStamp = null) : base(timeStamp) { }

        public override void SomeFunc() {}
    }

In c#, base class parameterized constructors are [not automatically inherited by derived classes](https://stackoverflow.com/q/2095749/344280), so you need to add the constructor explicitly.   Having done so, Newtonsoft will call the parameterized constructor, matching JSON property names to argument names as explained [here](https://stackoverflow.com/q/23017716) and [here](https://www.newtonsoft.com/json/help/html/JsonConstructorAttribute.htm).  But with neither a parameterized constructor nor a setter for `TimeStamp`, there's no possible way for Json.NET to set a value for it.

Demo fixed fiddle [here](https://dotnetfiddle.net/0J1GLV).

I'd recommend closing this.

