`v1`, `v2` and `v3` shares the same Values collection. At the end, the `Values` collection contains 3 elements (3 times `"Value1"`).

The reference of `public List<string> Values { get; set; } = VALUES;` is set even before the constructor is called.
Therefor the objects `Values` list is referencing the static list. And then the value gets added to the same list upon deserialization.

If you don't set this reference in the constructor, or property initializer, Json.Net would create a new non-static list. That would be similar to `public List<string> Values { get; set; } = new List<string>();`

